  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Dyeball (Pisolithus arhizus) is a unique fungus belonging to the Pisolithaceae family, characterized by its distinctive ball-shaped fruiting bodies filled with spore-containing pellets. This species is notable for its symbiotic relationship with tree roots and its historical use as a natural dye source.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pisoliths (Spore Pellets)",
      "description": "Interior contains small, round pellet-like structures (pisoliths) that are yellowish to reddish-brown, visible when the fruiting body is cut open",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruiting Body Shape",
      "description": "Forms distinctive spherical to pear-shaped, stalkless or short-stalked structures, typically 2-5 cm in diameter",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Color Progression",
      "description": "Exterior begins yellowish-brown and darkens to dark reddish-brown or black with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Dyeball thrives in sandy or disturbed soils in open woodlands, roadsides, and disturbed areas throughout New England, often forming mycorrhizal associations with hardwoods and conifers.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'Dyeball' refers to its historical use by dyers; the fruiting bodies produce a yellow to brownish dye that was utilized in textile production, particularly in Europe.",
